\section{Version}

We expose the package version as a module attribute so that callers --- the
save-format versioning in \texttt{persistence}, a future \texttt{--version}
flag, release tooling --- have a single authoritative source rather than
duplicating the literal.  It mirrors the version declared in
\texttt{pyproject.toml}.

<<constants>>=
__version__ = "0.1"
@

\section{Speaking the player's language}

[[maventyr]] is Swedish-first, but the curriculum itself sometimes demands
another language: an English topic is taught in English, so the characters
posing it must answer in English too.  Rather than bind each user-facing
string to one language, we store it as a \emph{constant directory} --- a
mapping from language code to text --- and resolve the active language only at
the moment we show it.  This keeps the Swedish and English wordings side by
side in the source, where a translator sees both at once and neither can drift
silently out of step with the other.

One language is privileged as the default.  Content is authored Swedish-first,
so a Swedish entry always exists while a translation may not yet; a lookup
against a missing language must therefore degrade to the default rather than
fail mid-game.  We name that default once, so the fallback rule below and
every [[language=]] parameter in the package draw on a single authority
instead of repeating the literal \enquote{sv}.

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_LANGUAGE = "sv"
@

The lookup encodes the fallback directly: ask the directory for the requested
language, and substitute the default's text when that key is absent.  Because
the default entry is assumed always present, a caller can localize a
half-translated directory without first guarding against a missing key --- the
fallback, not an exception, is the failure mode.

<<functions>>=
def localize(table, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the ``language`` text from a ``{language: text}`` mapping.

    Falls back to ``DEFAULT_LANGUAGE`` when ``table`` has no entry for
    ``language``, so a partially translated mapping still yields text.
    """
    return table.get(language, table[DEFAULT_LANGUAGE])
@

\section{A first, verifiable behaviour}

Even an empty game should be able to greet its player, and we use that
greeting to prove the build pipeline end to end: the chunk below tangles to
Python, the package imports, and the test that follows runs.  The welcome
message is our first constant directory --- the same Swedish text as before,
now paired with its English wording so the greeting honours the player's
language from the very first line the game shows.

<<constants>>=
WELCOME = {
    "sv": "Välkommen till Majas matematiska äventyr!",
    "en": "Welcome to Maja's mathematical adventure!",
}
@

The greeting resolves that directory through [[localize]], defaulting to
Swedish.  We route even this trivial string through the shared helper rather
than indexing the directory directly, so that every user-facing string ---
not just the ones a frontend happens to remember --- inherits the same
default-language fallback by construction.

<<functions>>=
def greeting(language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the game's welcome message in ``language``.

    Defaults to Swedish; an unknown language falls back to Swedish too.
    """
    return localize(WELCOME, language)
@

Let us verify that the package imports and that the greeting speaks the right
language --- the smoke test that guards the M0 scaffold.  We read the version
through the module object rather than the star-import, because [[*]] omits
dunder names like [[__version__]]; the public names [[greeting]], [[WELCOME]],
[[localize]] and [[DEFAULT_LANGUAGE]] come in through the star-import as usual.

<<test [[maventyr.py]]>>=
import maventyr
from maventyr import *


def test_has_version():
    assert isinstance(maventyr.__version__, str)


def test_greeting_is_swedish_welcome():
    assert greeting() == WELCOME["sv"]
    assert "äventyr" in greeting()
@

The greeting earns its [[language]] parameter only if a translation actually
changes the output and a missing one does not break it.  We check both halves:
an English greeting differs from the Swedish default, and an unknown language
resolves back to the default rather than raising.

<<test [[maventyr.py]]>>=
def test_greeting_translates_to_english():
    assert greeting("en") == WELCOME["en"]
    assert greeting("en") != greeting("sv")


def test_unknown_language_falls_back_to_default():
    assert greeting("xx") == greeting(DEFAULT_LANGUAGE)
@
